From: mscharged-port
Subject: Zero the native Variant pointer member before a narrow store
Upstream-base: 7a6bf165d9f1c5914181c079c7d4bfa7204f3509
Classification: native compiler/ABI compatibility

Variant(eVariantType, T) stores sizeof(T) bytes: one for a bool, four for
an int, u32, float or enum. On the Wii that fills the 32-bit pointer member,
so the original code may read a stored word back through mData.pointer, as
FielderDesireMachine::Update and ScriptState::Update did with update results
(patch 0690). Natively the pointer is eight bytes and bytes 4..7 stay
uninitialised; the copies that follow (FuzzyVariant::operator=, CopyFrom)
carry them along. That is the root cause of issues #25, #28 and #29.
DesireUpdate::operator=<int> in 1.0.7:
- Windows (Clang): movl %edx,0x30(%rsp) into the temporary, then the whole
  union is copied, so the result holds four stale stack bytes.
- macOS (Apple Clang): str w1,[sp,#0x18], then the same full copy.
- Linux (GCC): Reset() clears the union first, so the result is clean, but
  only because of that code generation.

Natively the constructor first clears the pointer member, as Reset already
does (patch 0587). A pointer-width read of a stored word then equals the Wii
word read. Values, types and all algorithms are unchanged. The Wii build is
unchanged.

diff --git a/include/Game/AI/Variant.h b/include/Game/AI/Variant.h
--- a/include/Game/AI/Variant.h
+++ b/include/Game/AI/Variant.h
@@ -50,6 +50,12 @@ public:
     template <typename T>
     Variant(eVariantType type, T value)
     {
+#if defined(MSCHARGED_NATIVE)
+        // A Wii word fills the whole 32-bit pointer member; natively the
+        // pointer is wider. Clear it first so a pointer-width read of a stored
+        // word sees that word, as on the Wii, instead of stale bytes.
+        mData.pointer = 0;
+#endif
         *(T*)&mData = value;
         mType = type;
     }
